<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahdia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mahdia is a port city on the east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, built on a narrow peninsula. Founded in 916 by the first Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, Abdallah al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>, it was the Fatimid capital until 948 and later the last stronghold of the Zirids after the Hilalian invasions. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> took it in 1148 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> in 1160, and Pisan, Genoese and French fleets attacked it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahdia.jpg" alt="Mahdia" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Davide Mauro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahdia_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mahdia is a Tunisian coastal city, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bitola">Monastir</a> and south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sousse">Sousse</a>, and the capital of Mahdia Governorate. Its old part corresponds to the Roman city called Aphrodisium and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, or Cape Africa. A bishopric called Africa is supposed to have been established when the city was held by the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, within the Kingdom of Africa from 1147 to 1160, and Pope Eugene III consecrated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> for it in 1148. A ship carrying Greek art treasures, dated to about 80 BCE and found sunk off the shore, is known as the Mahdia shipwreck.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Fatimid caliph Abdallah al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> founded the city and made it the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>. Since the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> was a Shi'a regime backed by a Berber Kutama army, he may have wished to put distance between his power base and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a>, a mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> city and the traditional capital. Construction began in 916, and the city was inaugurated on 20 February 921. It had heavy walls, the Fatimid palaces, an artificial harbour and the Great Mosque of Mahdia. Most of the Fatimid city has not survived, but the mosque is among the best-preserved Fatimid monuments in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>, though much of it was rebuilt by archaeologists in the 1960s.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zirid-dynasty">Zirid dynasty</a>, successors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>, moved their capital to Mahdia in 1057. In 1087 ships from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> raided it and burned the Muslim fleet in the harbour, which helped Christians take control of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and supply the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a> by sea. The city fell to the Norman conquest in 1148 and came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> rule in 1160. The role of capital passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> in the 12th century. In 1390 a French army besieged Mahdia in the Barbary Crusade and failed to take it.</p><p>The Spaniards captured the city in 1550. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> offered it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Order of Saint John</a>, who refused as too costly, and he ordered the Viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, Juan de Vega, to dismantle it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> then reoccupied it, but it lived only by fishing and oil-works and lost its commercial importance. It remained under Turkish rule until the 19th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The city, with 76,513 inhabitants, is a provincial centre north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sfax">Sfax</a> and is known for fish processing and weaving. Its railway station is the southern terminus of the Sahel Metro line from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sousse">Sousse</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bitola">Monastir</a>. The Great Mosque and fragments of mosaic pavements from the Fatimid palaces are what remain of its early history.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mahdia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1390 a French and Genoese expedition under Louis II, Duke of Bourbon, besieged <b>Mahdia</b> without success.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under his admiral George of Antioch Sicilian fleets captured Tripoli in 1146 and <b>Mahdia</b> in 1148, creating a short-lived dominion on the North African coast known as the Kingdom of Africa, which the Almohads overran by 1160, and in 1147 raided Corfu, Thebes and Corinth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Genoa was an old Ligurian port, squeezed between the Apennines and the sea, which had suffered Muslim raids in the tenth century and by the eleventh was raiding back, joining Pisa in attacks on Sardinia and on <b>Mahdia</b> in 1087.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Ali's brother Yahya ibn Ghaniya they took Tunis and <b>Mahdia</b> around 1203, devastating towns and countryside across Ifriqiya and Tripolitania.</blockquote><a href="/state/banu-ghaniya-of-ifriqiya">Banu Ghaniya of Ifriqiya</a></li><li><blockquote>His admiral George of Antioch took Tripoli in 1146, and in 1148 <b>Mahdia</b>, Sousse and Sfax fell as the Zirid ruler al-Hasan fled.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-africa">Kingdom of Africa</a></li><li><blockquote>Confined to <b>Mahdia</b> and the coast, the dynasty ended in 1148 when the Normans of the Kingdom of Africa took Mahdia.</blockquote><a href="/state/zirid-emirate">Zirid emirate</a></li></ul></section>
